<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Anointing of the Sick" cic="CCC 1499-1532" scripture="Jas 5:14-15; Mk 6:13; Lk 10:34">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after the lesson or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>Who was St. Camillus de Lellis before his conversion, and what transformed him?</li>
<li>According to the Letter of James (Jas 5:14-15), what should a Christian do when they are sick?</li>
<li>Why did the Second Vatican Council change the name from &laquo;Extreme Unction&raquo; to &laquo;Anointing of the Sick&raquo;?</li>
<li>Name at least three effects of this sacrament according to the Catechism (CCC 1520-1523).</li>
<li>What is Viaticum and what does it mean?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no &laquo;correct&raquo; answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. What image did you have of this sacrament before the lesson? Has anything changed?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Many people associate the Anointing with &laquo;the last thing&raquo; before dying. If you grew up with that idea, explain it. How do you feel now knowing it is a sacrament for the sick, not just for the dying?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Do you know someone who is going through a difficult illness or old age right now?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Write their name. How could you talk to them about this sacrament without frightening them? Mentally prepare a simple way to explain that the Anointing is Christ the physician coming to give them strength.</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="margin-top:8px">Name: ___________</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. The Catechism says that suffering, united to Christ's, can have redemptive value (CCC 1521). What does this mean for you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Think of a difficulty you are living through right now (it doesn't have to be physical illness: it could be a family problem, a sadness, a difficult situation). Could you offer that difficulty to God, praying for someone else? How would that make you feel?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. The parable of the Good Samaritan (Lk 10:34) shows someone who stops, bandages wounds, and pours on oil and wine. The Church Fathers saw in this an image of Christ and the sacraments.</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Who has been a &laquo;Good Samaritan&raquo; in your life when you were wounded —physically or emotionally?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. The Eucharist as Viaticum means &laquo;provision for the journey&raquo;: it is the bread that nourishes for crossing the frontier of death.</p>
<p class="question-block__p">If today were your last day on earth, what would you say to God? Write a short prayer (4-6 lines) in your own words.</p>
